## 1. VP, Portfolio Credit – Home & Auto — **9.2**

This remains the strongest match.

The role directly emphasizes portfolio credit management, consumer-credit strategy, risk appetite, credit-policy oversight, delinquency and loss mitigation, champion/challenger learning, client and executive presentations, pricing input, and SAS/Python analytics. fileciteturn115file1

Your direct advantages are unusually strong:

- Eight years in Wells Fargo Consumer Auto
- Four years of OCC portfolio credit-risk analysis
- Consumer Auto account and portfolio analysis across CPI, SCRA, disaster relief, COVID deferrals, and furnishing
- Decision Forum strategy and effective challenge
- Portfolio governance across 100M+ account-month actions
- Current credit-strategy simulation and challenger evidence
- Executive Director title and senior stakeholder credibility

The principal gaps remain:

- no direct retailer portfolio P&L ownership;
- no deal-renewal authority;
- limited direct fraud-strategy ownership;
- matrix leadership rather than formal performance-management responsibility.

The posting’s internal inconsistency—“Home & Auto” in the title, but Oil & Gas and Car Care clients in the body—does not reduce your fit materially, although it should be clarified during screening. fileciteturn115file1

## 2. VP, Portfolio Credit Manager — **9.0**

This is very close to the Home & Auto role and may ultimately be just as viable depending on the assigned client portfolio.

The role’s portfolio growth, risk appetite, policy, delinquency/loss management, pricing, client partnership, P&L outcomes, and executive communication mandate aligns strongly with your updated narrative. Your original application package already connected the position to governed credit strategies, portfolio analytics, customer impact, financial exposure, credit policy, and executive decisions. fileciteturn119file4

Why it sits slightly below 2602103:

- The general Portfolio Credit Manager mandate appears to lean more heavily toward direct client portfolio ownership, P&L, pricing, and commercial partner management.
- The Home & Auto position gives your eight years of Auto experience a more obvious domain bridge.

Still, this is a serious fit and should remain a top priority.

## 3. VP, Amazon Analytics — **8.9**

This role is highly aligned to your **enterprise analytics leadership**, but somewhat less directly aligned to your strongest credit-risk specialization.

Its focus includes:

- portfolio performance;
- lifecycle measurement;
- segmentation;
- forecasting;
- campaign and test-and-learn analytics;
- customer experience;
- executive and client recommendations. fileciteturn117file1

Your updated portfolio strengthens this application because you can now demonstrate:

- matched strategy comparisons;
- scenario and challenger testing;
- segmentation;
- executive Power BI evidence;
- customer-impact analytics;
- large-scale governed portfolio systems.

The remaining gaps are more meaningful than for the portfolio-credit positions:

- limited direct Amazon/retail partnership experience;
- limited production marketing-campaign measurement;
- limited acquisition-funnel and customer-lifecycle ownership;
- no direct retail-card P&L.

This remains an excellent broader analytics-leadership opportunity, but your credit-risk story is more differentiated in the first two positions.

## 4. AVP, Portfolio Credit Manager — **8.7**

This is a good substantive fit and may have a more attainable hiring threshold than the VP versions.

The strongest matches are:

- 12 years of portfolio credit-risk analytics;
- Auto and consumer-lending experience;
- credit-policy and risk-appetite judgment;
- portfolio monitoring and customer-impact analytics;
- executive recommendations;
- SAS, SQL, Python, Tableau, and Power BI;
- strategy simulation and Expected Loss analysis.

Why it ranks below the VP portfolio roles:

- The same direct portfolio P&L, retailer-client, pricing-authority, and fraud-strategy gaps remain.
- At AVP level, the role may be more executionally focused on day-to-day portfolio ownership, monthly performance management, and partner routines—areas adjacent to, but not identical to, your Wells Fargo remit.

It is nevertheless a credible application and not a down-level role in substance.

## 5. AVP, Credit Model Development — **8.6**

This is the strongest pure modeling position of the group, but it is not the best overall use of your complete leadership profile.

The role covers:

- hands-on model development;
- data collection and cleansing;
- customer account-management models;
- vendor-model review;
- validation and annual reviews;
- model-performance interpretation;
- regulatory documentation;
- Python, SAS, AWS, ML, Tableau, and automation. fileciteturn117file2

Your direct strengths include:

- Cox proportional hazards and logistic modeling;
- longitudinal credit-bureau panels;
- CECL and life-of-loan PD;
- model validation and effective challenge;
- vendor PD review;
- regulatory documentation;
- Python, SAS, SQL, Tableau, and Power BI;
- current survival and decision-model portfolio work.

The principal gaps are central:

- no production AWS/SageMaker or Spark experience;
- no recent full-time production model-developer role;
- limited model deployment and MLOps experience;
- no direct Account Management model ownership.

It remains Tier 1 because your OCC modeling foundation is genuine and substantial, but you would be competing against candidates whose recent positions were explicitly production model development.

## 6. AVP, Authentication Analytics & Strategy — **8.0**

This remains the weakest of the six, though still plausible.

Your transferable strengths include:

- consumer-credit risk analytics;
- anomalous-pattern detection;
- customer-impact analysis;
- strategy controls;
- governance;
- monitoring and escalation;
- decision-system architecture;
- SAS, SQL, Python, and Power BI;
- executive communication.

The material domain gaps are:

- no direct authentication-strategy ownership;
- no production identity-verification or login-risk analytics;
- no fraud-loss or authorization-strategy ownership;
- no device, behavioral biometric, digital-channel, or identity-network experience;
- no direct production fraud-model monitoring.

Your remediation and forensic analytics background provides a credible bridge, but authentication is a specialized operating domain. I would keep this active but not prioritize it over the five roles above.

